From c77ddc967438b53aa4570b8794988b65ac1d366d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 07:26:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08088/11362] The vision tower reads ARC colours above chance
 but far below exact-match, and the parser that measured it was scooping row
 numbers as colours

gemma-4-31B-it is multimodal and unsloth ships an mmproj, but the ARC induce
prompt has always serialised 64x64 grids as run-length-encoded ASCII. This gate
asks whether the vision tower could carry the grid instead. It cannot.

Best scheme is a 1024x1024 PNG at 16px/cell: 0.461 exact-match against the RLE
text control's 0.352, on 128 paired probes over a deliberately charitable grid
(8x8-cell solid blocks, 16 maximally-separated nameable colours). The 0.90 bar
that exact-match induction needs is not close, and the charitable grid is an
upper bound, so a real frame settles it a fortiori. At 8px and 1px the schemes
are not distinguishable from the 6.25% chance rate once batching is accounted
for. The tower does NOT reproduce to_ascii()'s 4/14 and 5/15 last-digit
collapse -- 12 such confusions across 512 probes, no worse than unrelated pairs
-- which is a clean negative on the hypothesis that motivated the gate.

Liveness is proven four ways, the decisive one differential: four byte-identical
text prompts paired with four different solid-colour images returned four
distinct correct colour names, which no silent text-only fallback can do.

An adversarial review then found six defects in the first artifact, all verified
here against the run's own recorded predictions before being applied:

- The parser's positional fallback matched every bare 1-2 digit number in a
  prose reply and took the first eight in 0..15 as answers, so row and column
  indices were scored as colours (54 of 128 probes have a row or column below
  16). Confirmed in the data: wrong predictions match an EARLIER probe's truth
  in the same batch far more often than a later one -- text_rle 27/23/16 at
  k=+1/+2/+3 against 5/5/4 at k=-1/-2/-3 -- and those hits sit almost entirely
  in the batches that scored <=2/8 (4.64/batch) rather than >=6/8 (0.00/batch).
  So the striking 8/8-vs-0/8 bimodality is substantially the parser losing
  alignment, and the previous artifact published that as a finding about the
  model's spatial indexing. Retracted. Every exact_match is now labelled a lower
  bound; the run cannot be re-scored because only parsed predictions were saved.
  Parser fixed, raw replies now persisted.
- Two of five McNemar p-values were hardcoded 0.0, impossible for an exact test
  (true values 4.59e-07 and 4.00e-08). All five now computed from the data with
  their min-reachable p. The discordant counts were correct.
- sample_size_justification claimed 128 probes on a real ka59 frame. No
  real-frame arm was ever measured and the staged grid was lp85, not ka59.
- The escalation was described as deliberately not run; the run log shows it was
  launched and killed mid-arm. The reasoning for stopping it stands.
- Probe-level Wilson CIs and McNemar assumed 128 independent probes, but they
  were asked in 16 batches (design effect 3.3-7.0, effective n 18-38). px16's
  interval is [0.21, 0.71], not [0.38, 0.55]. Cluster-robust stats added.
- can_supplement_text rode on a 0.25 bar that exists only in the builder, written
  after the numbers. Re-anchored on the pre-registered 6.25% chance rate tested
  against the cluster-robust lower bound -- a strictly harder test it still passes.

Net effect on the headline: images may SUPPLEMENT the exact text, never replace
it. The earlier "statistically indistinguishable from the text prompt" claim is
withdrawn -- p=0.103 cannot establish equivalence, and the two arms carry
different amounts of parser bias (excess forward-shift 49 for text vs 26 for
px16), so under correction text would plausibly gain more than px16 does.

